Subject: [#94-3-367] Wire Tapping Issue; Ruling Class vs Liberty

To:  Tim Starr and All Interested

Re:  Infinity Switches and Telephone Tapping Bill

This is not my area of expertise, but the apparently libertarian Full Disclosure
newspaper edited by Glen L. Roberts seems to have a wealth of data on such
topics, the most such data I have seen anywhere.  Available from First Amendment
Press, Box 67, Lowell, MI 48331--Editorial Address Box 903-FD29, Libertyville,
ILL 60048  Phone 1-800-633-3274; Fax 1-616-897-7222.  They advertise a great
many books and articles on similar subjects.

Re:  The International Society for Individual Liberty

Is this the same group founded by Don Ernsberger (sic), Jarret Wolstein, et. al.
back in the late 1960's or early 1970's with "International" added to the
beginning?  How can I obtain a sample issue of Freedom Network News?  You could
consider mailing one to me c/o T-M, 17336 Harper #616, Detroit, MI 48203

Re:  Libertarians vs. the Ruling Class

I have long felt that libertarians should give more thought to the problem that
the ruling class is highly organized against the progress of liberty through
such groups as the Trilateral Commission, Council on Foreign Relations, Royal
Institute of Interntional Affairs, Bilderbergers, etc.

I have not been active in any part of the movement since the Dallas Libertarian
Party Convention in 1974(?).  To my knowledge, at that time only Sam Konkin III
(Agorist Institute--New Libertarian Notes) and Murray Rothbard (tepidly) was
interested in a ruling class expose approach.  Do either of these people still
publish?  They both had newsletters when I was active.

Frankly, people have to be taught to ignore for the most part the arguments
againsts liberty that are issued by the Court Intellectuals of the ruling class.
The ruling class benefits by restrictions on liberty (regulation increases
industry entry costs and help keep prices and profits up for existing cartels)
and further restrictions are required to achieve their grandiose plans for a
World State.

Sure, I'm aware of the fallacy of ad hominem arguments, but during a war do we
totally ignore the motives of the enemy?  Unless we rely to some extent on the
ad hominem argument the enemy can take-up all our time and resources countering
fallacious arguments.  I suspect exposing the ruling class funding for the
myriad of environmentalist groups might be more effective.  World Watch is a
good example--funded by the big natural resource cartelists.  The World
Wilderness Conferences are another good example. . .actually attended by
Rockefeller and Rothschild.  Of course, the World Wildlife Fund is run by Prince
Philip.  His wife and the related Dutch House of Orange control a titantic share
of world resources through their holdings.  Little wonder Queen Juliana's
husband Prince Bernhardt helped organize the Bilderbergers.

Re:  Ruling Class/Conspiracy Research-Education Groups?

I have found the Project, the newsletter of A-albionic Research, PO Box 20273,
Ferndale, MI 48220 fax 1-313-885-1181 to be valuable on these issues.  They try
to stay away from ideology, but I suspect they are anarcho-capitalists at heart
or at least extreme individualist.

Please let me know of any significant Internet Mailing lists that touch on or
are devoted to ruling class/conspiracy issue.  I would prefer libertarian
leaning groups but right and left analysis can be useful also.

The CompuServe Conspiracy Theory Section in the Political Debate Forum is quite
useful.  I have uploaded a great deal of information there.

Re:  Electronic Mail Postman for A-albionic Research

As a Supporting Subscriber to A-albionic's Project, I have agreed to receive
their electronic mail for them.  Feel free to forward mail to them through me.
I forward them hard copies only, however.

Do not expect electronic responses, however.  They guarantee mail responses only
to Corresponding Subscribers and respond to others only if they are sufficiently
impressed and want to follow-up.  They do send out free info packs and say they
read all letters. They feel electronic mail takes-up too much time and want to
keep an "arm's length" from it.

